# Option 2 - Multi-Pod Distributed Processing

## a. Scheduler Execution

1.⁠ ⁠All application pods start simultaneously.
2.⁠ ⁠No ShedLock is used.
3.⁠ ⁠Every pod participates in processing.

---

## b. Batch Claim

1.⁠ ⁠Each pod atomically claims 100 Pending (⁠ STA201 ⁠) notifications.

⁠ sql
SELECT ...
FROM ntfy_trk
WHERE ntfy_sta_tx = 'STA201'
ORDER BY schd_ts
FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED
LIMIT 100;
 ⁠

2.⁠ ⁠Database locks only the claimed rows.
3.⁠ ⁠Other pods automatically skip locked rows.
4.⁠ ⁠Claimed rows are updated to ⁠ STA204 (Processing) ⁠ (or remain locked if using the long transaction approach).
5.⁠ ⁠Transaction is committed.

---

## c. Notification Processing

1.⁠ ⁠Each pod processes only its claimed notifications.
2.⁠ ⁠ExecutorService creates 5 worker threads.
3.⁠ ⁠Email retries are executed within the same processing flow.

---

## d. Status Update

1.⁠ ⁠Email Success → Update to ⁠ STA203 (Completed) ⁠.
2.⁠ ⁠Email Failure → Update to ⁠ STA205 (Failed) ⁠.

---

## e. Continue Processing

1.⁠ ⁠Pod atomically claims the next batch of 100 notifications.
2.⁠ ⁠Repeat until no Pending notifications remain.
3.⁠ ⁠Worker exits.

---

## f. Retry Processing

1.⁠ ⁠Separate scheduled job fetches ⁠ STA205 (Failed) ⁠ notifications.
2.⁠ ⁠Updates them back to ⁠ STA201 (Pending) ⁠ based on business policy.
3.⁠ ⁠Notifications become eligible for the next scheduled batch.

---

## Advantages

•⁠  ⁠Utilizes all pods.
•⁠  ⁠Automatic load balancing.
•⁠  ⁠No duplicate row claims.
•⁠  ⁠Horizontally scalable.
•⁠  ⁠Short database transactions.
•⁠  ⁠Industry-standard distributed worker pattern.

---

## Disadvantages

•⁠  ⁠Requires introducing ⁠ PROCESSING ⁠ and ⁠ FAILED ⁠ statuses.
•⁠  ⁠Requires recovery strategy for stale Processing records.
•⁠  ⁠Slightly higher implementation complexity.
